            <div class="col s12 m8 paragraphStyleNews">
                <h4 class="bolderFont">
                    Carla Aguilar López on Blue Shades, Authenticity, and Why Human Stories Matter More Than Ever.
                </h4>
                <p>From directing her own films to reporting from the world's most prestigious film festivals,
                    <span class="bolderFont">Carla
                        Aguilar López</span> has built a career that spans both sides of the camera. The Venezuelan
                    actress and
                    film
                    journalist, based in London, has steadily established herself as a storyteller across multiple
                    disciplines, with credits including The Undertone of David Jansen, Fantastic, Nondescript, and her
                    own short films—including VIDA, now available on Vilpa Max.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Her next role will see her lead <span class="bolderFont">Blue Shades,</span> Vilpa Films' upcoming
                    original animated series set in a
                    future where artificial intelligence dominates the entertainment industry and human creativity has
                    become a crime. As Elisa, Carla portrays a young artist forced to fight for her identity in a
                    society that no longer values human expression.
                </p>
                <p>We sat down with Carla to discuss the creative process behind Elisa, the importance of authenticity
                    in today's film industry, and why Blue Shades speaks directly to one of the defining conversations
                    of our time.</p>

                <hr>
                <h5 class="bolderFont">Finding Elisa</h5>
                <p>
                    For Carla, Elisa immediately stood out because of her resilience.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"The most interesting thing to me about her is
                        the fact that she is a rebel."</span>
                </p>
                <p>Rather than portraying someone defeated by the world around her, Carla was drawn to a character who
                    refuses to surrender despite being marginalized.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"She is strong, she is driven, she is smart,
                        she is determined, and she is human, which is a big deal in this story."</span>

                </p>
                <p>Preparing for the role meant immersing herself in worlds that explore resistance and identity under
                    oppressive systems. Carla revisited films such as V for Vendetta, Blade Runner 2049, and Nicolas
                    Winding Refn's Her Private Hell, studying how their protagonists balance vulnerability with
                    determination.</p>

                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"What drew me to all three were the way their
                        characters navigate oppressive or disorienting worlds, constantly shifting between resistance
                        and vulnerability, and I got inspired to portray Elisa that way."</span>

                </p>
                <hr>
                <h5 class="bolderFont">Building on The Undertone of David Jansen</h5>
                <p>Blue Shades marks Carla's second collaboration with director Alejandro Vilpa after portraying Ivana
                    in The Undertone of David Jansen. Looking back, she describes that experience as transformative.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"My experience was exquisite."</span>

                </p>
                <p>Playing a professional violinist without having a musical background became one of the greatest
                    challenges of her career.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"Preparation is everything and going into every
                        single detail of the character and of the script before shooting is key."</span>

                </p>
                <p>
                    That approach has become something she continues to carry into every new production.
                </p>


                <hr>
                <h5 class="bolderFont">A Community Built Around Artists</h5>
                <p>As one of the first members of <span class="bolderFont">Vilpa Talents,</span> Carla believes the
                    initiative offers something every
                    artist needs beyond career opportunities: belonging.
                </p>
                <p class="bolderFont">"It is a beautiful initiative because it gives you the sense of belonging, and I
                    feel that's very
                    important for an actor and for any human being actually."</span>

                </p>
                <p>She also believes visibility is one of the platform's greatest strengths.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"Vilpa Talents is still very new but I know
                        Alejandro, and he will make something really special out of this."</span>

                </p>
                <hr>
                <h5 class="bolderFont">Growing Through Opportunity</h5>
                <p>Reflecting on her career, Carla credits both Vilpa Films and Vilpa Max with giving her opportunities
                    that fundamentally changed her professional trajectory.</p>
                <p>Before joining The Undertone of David Jansen, much of her acting work came through background roles.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"I was doing a lot of background acting, which
                        gave no visibility at all."</span>

                </p>
                <p>Today, not only has she moved into leading roles, but one of her own directorial projects has found a
                    home on Vilpa Max.</p>

                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"I filmed my own short films as well, one of
                        them being VIDA that is currently on Vilpa Max—something I never thought would happen."</span>

                </p>
                <p>Looking ahead, her ambitions remain authentic.</p>

                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"I just want to keep acting."</span>

                </p>
                <p>And ultimately:</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"My goal in life is to be able to make a living
                        from acting and being able to live a thousand lives in this one."</span>

                </p>
                <hr>
                <h5 class="bolderFont">Authenticity Above Everything</h5>
                <p>Alongside acting, Carla has spent years covering international cinema as a journalist, attending
                    festivals including Cannes, Berlin and Venice.</p>
                <p>She believes those experiences have shaped her less as a performer and more as a professional.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"Once you reach the deepest layer of your
                        authenticity, you are able to show them what you want them to see, instead of molding yourself
                        into what you think they want to see."</span>

                </p>
                <p>For Carla, authenticity is not simply an artistic ideal—it is one of the industry's greatest
                    competitive advantages.</p>

                <hr>
                <h5 class="bolderFont">Why Blue Shades Feels So Relevant</h5>
                <p>Asked what audiences will connect with most, Carla immediately points to the series' central theme.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"I think AI is on everybody's mind right
                        now."</span>

                </p>
                <p>What makes Blue Shades compelling, she argues, is that its fictional world doesn't feel entirely
                    fictional.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"The fact that what happens in the series is
                        not that surreal... is something that can happen if we are not careful enough."</span>

                </p>
                <p>Rather than offering easy answers, she hopes the series will encourage audiences to continue debating
                    one of today's most urgent questions.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"I think the series will spark that debate a
                        little more."</span>

                </p>
                <hr>
                <h5 class="bolderFont">A Story About Humanity</h5>
                <p>At its heart, Carla sees Elisa's journey as a warning.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"We have created a monster and if we are not
                        careful we'll end up like Victor Frankenstein when the creature says to him: 'You created me but
                        I will be your master.'"</span>

                </p>
                <p>She hopes audiences leave the series asking difficult questions—not only about technology, but about
                    creativity itself.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"I would love people to ask themselves deeper
                        questions about AI related to art. Related to the soul."</span>

                </p>
                <hr>
                <h5 class="bolderFont">Advice for the Next Generation of Artists</h5>
                <p>As artificial intelligence continues reshaping creative industries, Carla believes the answer is
                    neither fear nor denial, but education.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"Knowledge is everything."</span>

                </p>
                <p>Rather than rejecting technology outright, she encourages artists to understand it well enough to use
                    it responsibly.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"We can understand how to use this tool to our
                        advantage instead of letting it destroy us completely."</span>

                </p>
                <p>But she also believes that technology alone will never determine the future.</p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont" style="font-style: italic;">"We need to detach ourselves from selfishness
                        if we want to do that and work hand in hand with a technology that can give us so much but that
                        can also take away from us so many things if we are not cautious."</span>

                </p>

